## Key Vietnamese Terms

| Term | English rendering | Context |
|------|------------------|---------|
| giang hồ | gangster / underworld / river pirate | Romanticized outlaw figure |
| du đãng | outlaw / hooligan | Pejorative/neutral |
| đại ca | boss / big brother | Gangster rank address |
| nối khố | sworn brother (lit. "sharing a loincloth") | Prison/underworld bond |
| kết nghĩa | swear brotherhood | Ritual fraternity |
| vượt ngục | prison break / escape | Specific to Con Dao escapes |
| cải lương | reformed theater | Vietnamese folk opera |
| "ăn thua đủ" | "go all the way" / "play for keeps" | Bay Vien's motto |
| nghĩa đệ | sworn younger brother | Fraternal relationship |
| cặp rằn | striped tiger / Khmer boss | Prison cellblock chief (c. 1930s Con Dao) |
| hàng tồn kho | "warehoused goods" | Political has-beens kept on ice |
| thầy chú | guard / overseer | Con Dao prison guards (from Sino-Vietnamese) |
| mã tà | native guard / trusty | Vietnamese guard/trustee in prison |
| Tạo Ebook: Nguyễn Kim Vỹ | (boilerplate, strip) | Vnthuquan.net ebook production line |
| Nguồn truyện: vnthuquan.net | (boilerplate, strip) | Source attribution, strip from extracted text |

## Language Notes

- **Honorifics**: Vietnamese kinship terms (anh, chị, ông, bà, cô, chú, etc.) used as pronouns — when they function grammatically as subjects/objects rather than meaningful relationship markers, render as plain "he/she/you/I" in English colloquial translation. Keep when the kinship IS narratively significant (e.g. "đại ca" → "big brother" as a title).
- **Đ vs Ð**: Vietnamese D with stroke. In PDF extractions, may appear as Ð (Latin capital letter D with stroke, sometimes encoded differently). Both are the same letter. Normalize to Đ when comparing against a reference title list.
- **Page header triplet**: Vietnamese ebook PDFs often repeat `[Author]` / `[Book Title]` / `[Chapter Title]` at the top of every leaf page. Strip the first two lines, keep the chapter title as a boundary marker.
- **Southern dialect**: Texts about the Bình Xuyen (based in Cho Lon/Saigon) use Southern Vietnamese colloquialisms. These sound distinct from Northern/Hanoi Vietnamese — the register is earthier and more direct. The translation register should match: conversational, not literary.
